  <div class="attributed-text-segment-list__container relative mt-1 mb-1.5 babybear:mt-0 babybear:mb-0.5">
    <p class="attributed-text-segment-list__content text-color-text !text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap break-words
        " dir="ltr" data-test-id="main-feed-activity-card__commentary">𝗦𝗙𝗖 𝘁𝗼 𝗲𝗻𝗵𝗮𝗻𝗰𝗲 𝗛𝗼𝗻𝗴 𝗞𝗼𝗻𝗴’𝘀 𝗿𝗲𝘁𝗮𝗶𝗹 𝗳𝘂𝗻𝗱 𝗿𝗲𝗴𝗶𝗺𝗲
 
The <a class="link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Ffeed%2Fhashtag%2Fsfc&amp;trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>#SFC</a> will introduce measures to strengthen the regulatory framework for SFC-authorised retail funds, as outlined in its consultation conclusions published today.
 
The enhancements to the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds (UT Code) will take effect in November 2026. They reflect the SFC’s commitment to investor protection and aligning Hong Kong’s regulatory framework with international standards.
 
Key changes include introducing a Value-at-Risk (VaR) approach to manage retail funds’ derivative exposure, supporting a wider range of fund strategies and products.
 
Other measures will streamline requirements for management company acceptability and feeder funds, giving fund managers greater operational flexibility. Enhanced safeguards for liquidity risk management and money market funds will also strengthen the resilience of Hong Kong’s retail fund market.
 
A 12-month transition period will generally apply to existing SFC-authorised funds from the effective date of the revised UT Code. The SFC will publish supplementary guidance, FAQs and revised templates in the coming months to support the transition.
 
Read the press release for details: <a class="link" href="https://lnkd.in/eW7yjhKH?trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" rel="nofollow" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>https://lnkd.in/eW7yjhKH</a></p>
