  <div class="attributed-text-segment-list__container relative mt-1 mb-1.5 babybear:mt-0 babybear:mb-0.5">
    <p class="attributed-text-segment-list__content text-color-text !text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap break-words
        " dir="ltr" data-test-id="main-feed-activity-card__commentary">At today’s 10th Annual Institute on Corporate &amp; Securities Law in Asia, Mr Michael Duignan, <a class="link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Ffeed%2Fhashtag%2Fsfc&amp;trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>#SFC</a> Executive Director of Enforcement, delivered a keynote address titled “A New Enforcement Philosophy: Less Trodden Pathways to Better Outcomes,” arguing that the SFC should pursue whichever lawful, workable solution delivers the fairest outcome fastest, rather than reaching for the same process out of habit. 

Mr Duignan argued that enforcement works best when it fixes the structural drivers of misconduct before a case begins. He pointed to the SFC’s January circular to licensed corporations carrying out sponsor work, issued amid a more than 200% year-on-year rise in Hong Kong IPO proceeds. Firms were given clear deadlines to report principal-to-deal ratios and flag unqualified staff, well before problems could surface as enforcement cases.

He also discussed persistent intermediary misconduct at a brokerage, where cross-divisional action, licensing conditions, and the prospect of disciplinary proceedings led the firm to voluntarily surrender its licence and wind down, avoiding a drawn-out hearing process while protecting the market.

Turning to minority shareholders, he highlighted the SFC’s settlements with PricewaterhouseCoopers Hong Kong ($1 billion) and Giordano International Limited (payments may be up to $1.5 billion). Both cases delivered redress to minority shareholders sooner than years of tribunal or court proceedings might have.

Mr Duignan also addressed the SFC’s power to direct trading suspensions for companies with unresolved concerns regarding their financial and operational status, describing it as a protection for the market as a whole. 
He made it clear that the new enforcement philosophy is not against process, but against treating one particular process as the only legitimate route to a good outcome.

Read the full speech here: <a class="link" href="https://lnkd.in/gvWuRfhq?trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" rel="nofollow" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>https://lnkd.in/gvWuRfhq</a></p>
<!---->  </div>



        

        
            
      
    
    
    

